    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter the July 24 slate with a balanced offensive outlook, led by the high-upside pairing of **Yordan Alvarez** (74.93) and **Christian Yelich** (74.33). **Yordan Alvarez** remains our premier offensive engine, boasting an elite xwOBA of 0.478, which shields his value even against a mid-tier matchup. Meanwhile, **Christian Yelich** benefits from a massive 19% boost in SP skill efficiency against Sugano, capitalizing on a favorable platoon split that elevates his floor significantly. We are also monitoring the middle infield, where **Caleb Durbin** (67.91) continues to be an underrated asset; his ability to exploit Yesavage\u2019s 4.70 xERA makes him an essential start, particularly given our organizational emphasis on speed and efficiency in 5x5 Roto formats.\n\nWhile the lineup is largely solidified, the decision to bench **Carson Benge** (51.93) was the most difficult call of the morning. **Carson Benge** posted a respectable score and actually outperformed **Dillon Dingler** (41.89) and **Kyle Schwarber** (50.54) in raw projection, but the optimizer prioritized the positional scarcity and specific lineup slots of our primary starters. We opted to keep **Kyle Schwarber** in the mix despite his tougher matchup against Schlittler, as the sheer run-production potential of his #2 spot in the order outweighs the marginal gains offered by bench depth. \n\nRisk management remains a priority today. **Dillon Dingler** is our most volatile start, sitting just above the 40.0 floor due to a negative platoon split against Way. Additionally, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (60.11) is a strong play, we have accounted for a 2% deduction due to the opposing catcher's elite 1.89s pop time, which creates a slight friction point for his stolen base upside. With **Hyeseong Kim** officially confirmed in the minors, our roster depth is locked; we are counting on the veterans **Junior Caminero** and **Lars Nootbaar** to anchor the heart of the lineup and maintain our pace in the Roto standings.",
